---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { digitalIdentities } from "../identifiers.ts";
import LayoutGlobal from "../layouts/LayoutGlobal.astro";
import { profile } from "../utilities/microdata.ts";
import { asCanonicalUrl } from "../utilities/urls.ts";

const profiles = (await getCollection("profiles")).sort((a, b) =>
  a.data.sortKey.localeCompare(b.data.sortKey),
);

const linkedIn = digitalIdentities.find(
  (d) => d.type === "social" && d.name.toLowerCase() === "linkedin",
)!;
const bluesky = digitalIdentities.find(
  (d) => d.type === "social" && d.name.toLowerCase() === "bluesky",
)!;
---

<LayoutGlobal
  meta={{
    canonical: "/about",
    title: "About Adam Coster",
    description:
      "Profiles, social media handles, and other digital identifiers for Adam Coster.",
    social: {
      image: asCanonicalUrl("/profile.portrait.1372.jpg"),
    },
    ldjsons: [profile],
  }}
>
  <h1>About Me</h1>

  <menu>
    <li>
      <a href="#profiles">Jump to Profiles</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="#identities">Jump to Digital Identities</a>
    </li>
  </menu>

  <p>
    I enjoy operating under uncertainty and exploring its boundaries, and have a
    knack for finding alternative explanations for observations that at first
    seem clear-cut. I also have a knack for figuring out <i>
      how to figure things out
    </i>: identifying what is and isn't known, making the unknown known, and
    deciding how to proceed after running into the unknowable.
  </p>
  <p>
    All of that proved to be quite useful over my foray into biomedical science
    (I've got a PhD in Cell & Molecular Biology), and more useful than you might
    expect once I changed careers to run a video game studio. It turns out that
    the edges of science are no more uncertain than the process of making and
    selling video games.
  </p>
  <p>
    My brothers and I co-founded <a href="https://www.bscotch.net"
      >Butterscotch Shenanigans</a
    > ("<abbr title="Butterscotch Shenanigans">Bscotch</abbr>"), a video game
    studio, in 2012. I joined full time in 2014 right after defending my
    dissertation. And we're somehow still here, making games, over a decade
    later. If you want to know more about that journey, we've been documenting
    it with a long-running <a href="https://podcast.bscotch.net"
      >weekly podcast called "Coffee with Butterscotch"</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bscotch was just the three of us when it started and has always had fewer
    than 10 total staff. So I've had to learn how to do a lot of things to let
    us stay independent in what is an extremely volatile industry. I built the
    web service we use for player accounts (called "Rumpus"), the web
    infrastructure we use to manage internal development data, a bunch of <a
      href="https://github.com/bscotch"
    >
      open source stuff related to the game engine we use (GameMaker)</a
    >, localization pipelines, art pipelines, a bespoke project management web
    service, and on and on. Right now I'm working on what will be our first
    public non-game software, <a href="https://ludokit.com">LudoKit</a>, which
    takes a bunch of that stuff I was doing just for Bscotch and makes it
    available to other game developers and publishers.
  </p>
  <p>
    When I'm not building software I'm doing a million other things to keep
    Bscotch running: strategy, business development, contract negotiation,
    project management, team management, data analytics, research... We try to
    keep everything in-house, so I wear a lot of hats.
  </p>
  <p>
    And when I'm not working, I'm probably lounging on the couch with my wife
    and cats, watching a TV show and having a cocktail. Or perhaps working on
    this very site.
  </p>

  <section>
    <header>
      <h2 id="profiles">Profiles</h2>
      <p>
        Below are profiles of varying length, written in 3rd person, so that
        anyone who needs one can pick whichever fits and copy it directly.
      </p>
    </header>
    <ul>
      {
        profiles.map((profile) => (
          <li>
            <article>
              <h3>{profile.data.title}</h3>
              <Fragment set:html={profile.rendered?.html} />
            </article>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <header>
      <h2 id="identities">Digital Identities</h2>
      <p>
        To make sure you can verify that some digital identifier, page, or
        profile you find out on the Internet is actually mine, I've tried to
        gather them all up to list below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that I don't regularly use most of these: if you're looking to
        connect your best bet is
        <a href={bluesky.url}>Bluesky</a> or <a href={linkedIn.url}>LinkedIn</a> (infrequently
        checked).
      </p>
    </header>
    <ul>
      {
        digitalIdentities.map((identity) => (
          <li>
            <a href={identity.url}>
              {identity.name}
              {"handle" in identity && `(as ${identity.handle})`}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
</LayoutGlobal>
